From 14958e425a75e0307d78cd546ebec8a15ba21fc5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CipherScope <dev@cipherscope.io>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 05:46:07 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 23/28] doc: record the P2MR deployment gate and the Schnorr
 sighash convention

Describe why importdescriptors and deriveaddresses refuse a tmr() descriptor
where the deployment is disabled, and what stays available there: parsing,
checksums and loading a wallet that already holds one. Note that P2MR follows
the Taproot sighash convention in PSBTs, that only control blocks matching the
output are used for signing, and that size estimation asks whether the spend
uses the witness rather than what address type it has.
---
 doc/descriptors.md |  2 +-
 doc/p2mr.md        | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/doc/descriptors.md b/doc/descriptors.md
index 0d8f2ba..4d73fa7 100644
--- a/doc/descriptors.md
+++ b/doc/descriptors.md
@@ -67,7 +67,7 @@ Descriptors consist of several types of expressions. The top level expression is
 - `multi_a(k,KEY_1,KEY_2,...,KEY_N)` (only inside `tr`): k-of-n multisig script using OP_CHECKSIG, OP_CHECKSIGADD, and OP_NUMEQUAL, defined in [BIP 387](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0387.mediawiki).
 - `sortedmulti_a(k,KEY_1,KEY_2,...,KEY_N)` (only inside `tr`): similar to `multi_a`, but the (x-only) public keys in it will be sorted lexicographically, defined in [BIP 387](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0387.mediawiki).
 - `tr(KEY)` or `tr(KEY,TREE)` (top level only): P2TR output with the specified key as internal key, and optionally a tree of script paths, defined in [BIP 386](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0386.mediawiki).
-- `tmr(TREE)` (top level only): **provisional**. P2MR output ([BIP 360](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/620871a7a442e276a058b487cd8743775fb499a4/bip-0360.mediawiki) draft) committing to the given tree of script paths. `TREE` uses the same syntax as in `tr()`, and its leaves are parsed and executed as BIP 342 tapscripts with leaf version `0xc0`, but there is no internal key and therefore no key path: the witness program is the script tree Merkle root itself. BIP 360 does not define a descriptor language, so this name and syntax are a placeholder for this build and may change; see [p2mr.md](p2mr.md). The tree must have at least two leaves, because a leaf at depth zero produces an `m = 0` control block, which BIP 360 accepts without executing the leaf script, making the output anyone-can-spend. Use `addr(ADDR)` or `raw(HEX)` if you need to express such an output. `tmr()` has no address type of its own, so it cannot be an active descriptor and the wallet never generates P2MR addresses or change for it.
+- `tmr(TREE)` (top level only): **provisional**. P2MR output ([BIP 360](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/620871a7a442e276a058b487cd8743775fb499a4/bip-0360.mediawiki) draft) committing to the given tree of script paths. `TREE` uses the same syntax as in `tr()`, and its leaves are parsed and executed as BIP 342 tapscripts with leaf version `0xc0`, but there is no internal key and therefore no key path: the witness program is the script tree Merkle root itself. BIP 360 does not define a descriptor language, so this name and syntax are a placeholder for this build and may change; see [p2mr.md](p2mr.md). The tree must have at least two leaves, because a leaf at depth zero produces an `m = 0` control block, which BIP 360 accepts without executing the leaf script, making the output anyone-can-spend. Use `addr(ADDR)` or `raw(HEX)` if you need to express such an output. `tmr()` has no address type of its own, so it cannot be an active descriptor and the wallet never generates P2MR addresses or change for it. It can only be imported, and addresses can only be derived from it, on a chain where the P2MR deployment is enabled; elsewhere such an output is anyone-can-spend.
 - `addr(ADDR)` (top level only): the script which ADDR expands to, defined in [BIP 385](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0385.mediawiki).
 - `raw(HEX)` (top level only): the script whose hex encoding is HEX, defined in [BIP 385](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/blob/master/bip-0385.mediawiki).
 - `rawtr(KEY)` (top level only): P2TR output with the specified key as output key. NOTE: while it's possible to use this to construct wallets, it has several downsides, like being unable to prove no hidden script path exists. Use at your own risk.
